    [ ] hash flags ???
    [ ] in-memory index:
        GId, CId, Attributes, WhiteSig, BlackSig, <pointer to MoreGame row>
        [x] equi-join Game->MoreGame
            no need for a hash table if both indexes are sorted identically
        [x] pull out CId -> [row..row[
            [x] Interval, IntervalSet
        [x] GId can be retrieved late from MoreGame
        [x] squeeze hasVariations into msb of pointer
         -> [x] index entry has only 24 bytes: WhiteSig,BlackSig, union{ row ptr, data ptr }
            24 * 16M = 384 MB
        [x] unit test (small, huge)
    [ ] Parallelistion
        [x] omp (for Windows)
        [x] my_thread_init. Table is not thread-safe
        [x] works in principle but doing index scan, file read, replay
            all at once is too much (for the caches)
            - scanning the index is fast (1s) single-threaded is best
            - reading rows (mi_rrnd) takes abt 10 seconds for 5M rows
            - replaying (BinReader::read) takes abt 8 seconds for 5M rows,
                on a warmed up cache
            -> 20 s on a cold cache
            >  10 s on a warm cache
            compare to 14 s with TCP+Java !?!?!

            we need more insights on:
            [-] (1) early-cut-off scan in parallel
                [x] omp for (static, dynamic?)
                [x] single-threaded is fastest
                [ ] put rowptrs in a separate vector (for better cache layout)
                [ ] put HasVariations into a std::bitset, or hash set of int ?
            [ ] (2) loading blobs from file
                [x] re-create _mi_read_dynamic_record
                    [x] reads from mmap, copies to recbuf, "unpacks" to output buf. Too many copies!
                    [x] get rid of _mi_unpack ?
                    [x]   don't memcpy, just compute offsets of some columns
                [x] refactor index to use DynRec columns
                [ ] use mmapped pointers for index read, too
                [ ] how many 'block's are needed for one record?
                    check with blobs > 64kb
            [x] (3) replay in parallel
                mmapped reads:
                11s for 5M games (cold cache)
                6-9s (warm cache)

    [-] Java interface (JNI): java.sql.ResultSet
        [-] thread-safe queue for results
        [ ] read/write batches (start with 1, increase to 10, 100, 1000, ...)
        [-] ResultSet.next() blocks/polls until new results arrive in queue
    [-] simpler JNI interface:
        [ ] build index, invalidate, check version
        [ ] start, cancel pos query
        [ ] callback resultsFound(int[]) posts into event loop.
            No need for ResultSet and Queue.
    [x] command-line interface

