==== Interesting New Features

* FOP printing: subtitles for Events and Rounds
    Q: sort by Collection, or by Event name (needs to be curated accurately) ?
    - drop info from Game header

[ ] Move Games to Collection.
    Useful for combining a bunch of pgn files (like Lubra's files)
    everything is there:
        GameSource src = GameSource.collectionContents(C1);
        MoveTask move (src, C2);
    just needs to be wired to a menu entry

* Bulk edit with F4
    [ ] GameSource is the abstraction
        [ ] define GameSource by SearchRecord where-clause
        [ ] use where-clause for dialog field filling
        [ ] use where-clause for bulk update
        [ ] in-memory GameSource has no where-clause, but a Game pointer
    [x] Autocomplete/suggest for Player, Event, Site, Opening (more?)
    [x] auto-complete in Search Panel
        [x] sql completer without constraint
        [x] distinct not needed (b/c Player is already distinct)
        [ ] with optional constraints (GameSource)
        [x] wildcards (query differs from result)
        [x] case insensitive -> for free (collate ut8_ci !)
        [ ] bug: extra char appears

    [x] fix: LIKE and RLIKE with accents
        search "Schafer","Schäfer,H." "Schafer,H."
        LIKE is accent-insensitive
        RLIKE is accent-sensitive
        BINARY loses accent-insenstivity
        -> LIKE, vs LIKE AND RLIKE return different results
        [+] use always LIKE AND RLIKE
            [x] LIKE is fast and matches letters (case-(in)-sensitive, accent-(in)-sensitive)
            [x] RLIKE matches punctuation [[:punct:][:white:]]
            [+] RLIKE is not accent-sensitive b/c accented chars are usually utf-8
                and RLIKE **does not** support utf-8
                (although the table collation **is** utf8; goodness, what have you been thinking at mysql??)
                poor workaround: character class placeholder [[:alpha:]]{1,3} matches one utf-8 letter
            [-] RLIKE prevents accent-sensitive matches from LIKE. That's nasty.
            [-] MySQL 8 has 'regexp_like(Name, 'Sch[=a=]fer.*' collate utf8mb4_0900_ai_ci);'
                [x] solves the utf-8 problem; [:alnum:] actually matches one letter
                [-] does not solve the accent match problem
            [x] use same query for Completer

            [x] java.text.Normalizer + Regex in prefixLength(); remove GlobX matcher
            [x] install grant tables
            [x] port stripDiacritics to C++
            [x] load UDF stripDiacritics
            [x] stripDiacritics + Regex in SQL
            [x] nasty (again): strip_diacritics() loses string collation. Use with care.

        [-] use only LIKE as fallback until there are UDFs for all platforms.
            [x] accent-insensitivity is preserved
            [x] use _% to match punctuation and white space.
                not matched exactly, but no matter.
            [x] BINARY is case and accent-sensitive. use simple LIKE as prefilter

    [ ] Find & Replace. Idea: use search, then Bulk Edit to edit just some fields
        search "Sch�fer, P.", replace with "Sch�fer, Peter"
        [ ] query to glob pattern (_%)
        [ ] map glob pattern groups to replacement patterns. //somehow//
        [ ] -> update only modified groups

    [ ] have one base Edit Panel for: Search Panel, Single Edit F4, Bulk Edit
        [ ] use Completer mechanism to fill in fields
            -> here's a synergy between autocomplete and bulk edit
        [ ] mark edited fields
        [ ] smart update: new Player row, or edit old in place?
    [ ] 4 modes of operation
        [ ] One Game object in memory
        [ ] One Game in DB
        [ ] Set of Games from DB; show only distinct fields?
        [ ] Result Set; possibly with wildcard fields
    [ ] re-use Search Panel for Single Game Edit


